      <h1>⭐ Morning Briefing — November 3, 2026</h1>

      <p class="briefing-subtitle">(Tuesday, November 3, 2026)</p>

      <p class="top-story"><strong>Top Story:</strong> TCEQ records show Core Scientific ran eight backup generators at its Denton AI campus for months this spring, reporting more nitrogen oxides in under three months than its permits allow the whole site in 15 years. The state has opened enforcement, and city staff briefed Denton's council on the case Friday as it weighs a moratorium.</p>

      <hr class="section-divider" />

      <h2>📰 Today's News</h2>

      <h3>1. 🏭 TCEQ Cites Core Scientific's Denton Campus After Backup Generators Ran Hundreds of Hours Past Their Limits</h3>

      <ul>
        <li>TCEQ sent Core Scientific a notice of enforcement on July 24 over generators at its Denton AI data center, the Denton Record-Chronicle reported Monday night, Nov. 2. City staff briefed council on the case Friday, Oct. 30.</li>
        <li>The company told TCEQ in late April it had found "a systemic internal manufacturing defect affecting ninety-three transformers." While it fixed them, it ran emergency generators instead. TCEQ investigator Luke Jones's July 22 report says four generators ran for that reason, and four more ran during what the records call a non-emergency event, from April 9 to June 23.</li>
        <li>Over those 11 weeks, the eight generators reported 384.13 tons of nitrogen oxides, 50.10 tons of carbon monoxide and 2.51 tons of particulate matter. The certified annual limits for every generator on the site combined are 24.9 tons of nitrogen oxides, 8.17 tons of carbon monoxide and 0.85 tons of particulate matter.</li>
        <li>The permit allows 100 hours a year of non-emergency running. Four generators at one building logged 1,352 to 1,371 hours, and four at another logged 527 to 1,024. Jones asked for records in April and followed up May 7, May 18 and May 26. The records he got were incomplete, and the violation notes that "no emissions calculations were provided" for diesel fuel storage.</li>
        <li>Mayor Pro Tem Nick Stevens listed the alleged violations online: exceeding emission rates and hour limits, failing to get a federal major-source air permit, and failing to keep compliance records. Denton County is in the Dallas–Fort Worth ozone area that EPA classifies as in severe nonattainment.</li>
        <li>TCEQ can settle through an agreed order or refer the case to the attorney general for civil penalties. No penalty amount has been reported. Core Scientific didn't respond to the paper by Monday evening. The Jim Christal Road campus is nearing full buildout on a contract for up to 394 MW.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="signal"><strong>Today's Signal:</strong> Core Scientific's campus is exempt from the moratorium Denton is considering, so the city can't use that vote to do anything about this. Denton also has no local air ordinance, so whatever happens next is up to TCEQ's Fort Worth regional office.</p>

      <h3>2. 📉 Fermi's TensorWave Lease Misses Its Oct. 31 Closing Date and Isn't in Effect Yet</h3>

      <ul>
        <li>Fermi said Tuesday, Nov. 3, that the lease between its subsidiary Fermi Campus 1 LLC and TensorWave TEX1 LLC "has not yet become effective" because its closing conditions weren't fully met by Oct. 31. That date had already been pushed back once, and this time it passed without another extension.</li>
        <li>The conditions still open include signed work letters and guaranties, final operations schedules and a service-level agreement, board approvals, and "project-level financing sufficient to fund construction of the leased premises." Neither side has moved to terminate.</li>
        <li>CEO Lee McIntire said both companies "remain committed to this lease" and that Fermi expects to say more on its earnings call next week "or sooner if appropriate." The lease was announced in August as a $6.5 billion deal and was Project Matador's first tenant.</li>
        <li>TradingView reported that Fermi shares fell 3.4% in premarket trading Tuesday (headline/snippet only). The miss comes four days after shareholders failed to reelect former Gov. Rick Perry to the board.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="signal"><strong>Today's Signal:</strong> The condition that matters is financing. Until there's money to build TensorWave's space, Matador's anchor tenant is still an agreement on paper, and that's the tenant Fermi has pointed to when telling investors the Panhandle campus has demand.</p>

      <h3>3. ⚛️ Vistra Answers PUC Staff: Write Our Commitments Into the Order, but Still Rule the Deal Lawful</h3>

      <ul>
        <li>Comanche Peak Power Company filed rebuttal testimony from Vistra development SVP Claudia Morrow in Docket 59399 at 4:28 p.m. Monday, Nov. 2, three days after PUC staff recommended approving the Amazon net metering arrangement only with ERCOT's conditions attached.</li>
        <li>Morrow said the applicants always expected the final order to require them to keep the commitments in their application and testimony, as it would in other dockets where a commitment was material to approval. She said that "with that clarification," ERCOT's concern "should be resolved."</li>
        <li>Morrow agreed with staff that under PURA § 39.169(c) an approved net metering arrangement can't later be attacked with a "reasonable cause objection." She still asked for an explicit finding that the arrangement is lawful, saying Comanche Peak and Amazon "benefit from an explicit finding" before "investing additional capital."</li>
        <li>ERCOT and the applicants also filed their exhibit lists Monday. The case is being decided on written filings, with no hearing.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="signal"><strong>Today's Signal:</strong> Vistra didn't accept ERCOT's conditions as written. It offered to make its own promises binding instead. If staff was right that Freestone and Crusoe Goodnight should be the template, commissioners will have to choose between ERCOT's wording and Vistra's.</p>

      <h3>4. 🔌 Cipher Gets a Conditional 1 GW Grid Slot at Colchis and Starts Building Gas Laterals for Off-Grid Sites</h3>

      <ul>
        <li>Cipher Digital reported Monday, Nov. 2, that its Colchis site received a conditional base-load classification of 1 GW through ERCOT's Batch Zero process. CEO Tyler Page told analysts the company has "fully complied with the data center audit" and expects final classification in December. Colchis has grown to 622 acres, and Page called a 1 GW grid-connected site "an extraordinarily rare thing."</li>
        <li>Three other Cipher sites, Mikeska, Apollo and the Stingray expansion, received Provisional Controllable Load Resource designations. Page said they were "3 of only 11 initial sites" to get one out of roughly 150 conditional study sites. The designation lets a site draw full power before local transmission upgrades are finished, with part of its load subject to curtailment.</li>
        <li>In the third quarter, Cipher started developing lateral gas pipelines that could supply up to 2.5 GW of on-site generation, with first power possible in 2027. Page said the company is in "early but active commercial discussions" for about 1 GW of leases at those sites, with independent power producers owning and running the generation.</li>
        <li>Cipher also bought a 70 MW Texas site called Horizon, which has a signed utility interconnection agreement and is expected to energize in 2029. At 70 MW, it's below ERCOT's 75 MW large-load threshold. Cipher's Barber Lake lease with an unnamed AI lab now totals about $9.1 billion over 20 years. The company didn't name counties for any of these sites.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="signal"><strong>Today's Signal:</strong> Cipher is working all three ways a Texas data center can get power right now: through the Batch Zero queue, by staying under the 75 MW threshold, and by building its own gas generation. Only the queue route waits on the audit, and Cipher says it has already complied and expects a final answer in December.</p>

      <h3>5. 🏙️ Dallas Planners Draft Data Center Rules With Size Tiers, Water-Free Cooling and Noise Studies</h3>

      <ul>
        <li>Dallas planning staff have drafted standards for data centers, CandysDirt reported Tuesday, Nov. 3. The City Council's Economic Development Committee was set to take them up Monday but deferred the item to its next meeting. Staff's presentation was released anyway.</li>
        <li>Today, Dallas treats data centers as "communications exchange facilities," allowed by right in many industrial, commercial, office, mixed-use and downtown districts. Staff would rename the category "Data and Communications Exchange Facility," name data centers in it explicitly, and exclude crypto mining.</li>
        <li>The draft sorts facilities into four sizes: 50,000 square feet or less, 50,000 to 500,000, 500,000 to 1 million, and 1 million or more. Smaller adaptive-reuse, brownfield and infill projects could stay by right. Greenfield projects, and any facility of 1 million square feet or more, would have to meet extra standards.</li>
        <li>Those standards include closed-loop or other water-free cooling, a 72-hour noise study before construction and another after, 30-foot landscaped buffers next to homes, and acoustic enclosures at least 1.5 times the height of any outdoor equipment within 250 feet of a property line. Staff plans to move these amendments separately from the city's broader zoning overhaul.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="signal"><strong>Today's Signal:</strong> Dallas is writing rules for the facilities it doesn't have yet. Reusing an old building, the way most of the city's data centers have been built, would still be allowed by right. The extra rules land on new campuses on open land, the kind of project now fighting for space at the edges of Dallas–Fort Worth.</p>

      <p>TCEQ's Nov. 4 commissioners' agenda has no data center, gas plant or Core Scientific items. Its only items in the tracked counties are wastewater and drinking-water cases in Wise, Hood, Denton and Tarrant. Monday's PUC filings show nothing new in the Skybox or Medina County Power Batch Zero cases. ERCOT hasn't posted a news release since Sept. 21, and nothing new was reported on ServerDome's Hudspeth campus.</p>

      <hr class="section-divider" />

      <h2>📍 Today's Watch List</h2>

      <ul>
        <li>Core Scientific: whether TCEQ settles with an agreed order or refers the case to the attorney general, what penalty it seeks, and whether Denton's council takes it up before its moratorium vote, as early as Dec. 1.</li>
        <li>Election Day today, Nov. 3: the governor's race between Greg Abbott and Gina Hinojosa, and whether the result changes how long the data center permit pause lasts.</li>
        <li>Fermi's earnings call next week, and whether the TensorWave lease closes, gets a new deadline or is terminated. Fermi's annual meeting vote tallies are due at the SEC around Nov. 5.</li>
        <li>Docket 59399: whether commissioners adopt ERCOT's conditions as written or Vistra's commitment-based approach, and whether they grant the lawfulness finding. Vistra reports third-quarter results in early November.</li>
        <li>PUC open meeting Nov. 5, including Item 40 on publishing the draft standard large-load interconnection agreement and staff's request for a common process for Batch Zero appeals.</li>
        <li>Dallas: the Economic Development Committee's next meeting, when the deferred data center standards come back.</li>
        <li>Cipher's Colchis final classification, which the company expects in December.</li>
        <li>ERCOT: Wholesale Market Subcommittee Nov. 4, Reliability and Operations Subcommittee Nov. 5, SB 6 large-load curtailment workshop #2 Nov. 6, Planning Working Group Nov. 9, Batch/CTP workshops Nov. 17 and 19, TAC Nov. 18, Large Load Working Group Nov. 19.</li>
        <li>Skybox's Batch Zero case is paused until Nov. 10. Still pending: a ruling on Medina County Power's appeal.</li>
        <li>PUC open meeting Nov. 19, with Docket 60368 interventions due the same day. ERCOT's calendar also lists a PUC meeting Dec. 3, the same day Austin could vote on its Title 4 changes.</li>
        <li>Comments on the draft large-load interconnection agreement are due Dec. 14. Chevron's final investment decision on its West Texas Microsoft project is due by the end of 2026.</li>
        <li>ERCOT audit reports are due Dec. 10, and the PUC meets Dec. 17.</li>
      </ul>

